        "topic_names": [
          "4.2 VERSATILE NATURE OF CARBON"
        ],
        "answer": "Structural isomers are compounds that possess the identical molecular formula but different structural arrangements of their atoms. For butane, C4H10, atoms can be connected in two distinct ways to form the 'carbon skeleton'. One structure consists of a single straight chain of four carbon atoms (n-butane). A second structure is formed by branching, where a central carbon atom is bonded to three other carbon atoms (isobutane). Filling the remaining valencies with hydrogen confirms that both configurations represent two unique structures for the same formula, C4H10.",
